specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md-400-Semantics:
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md-401-
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:402:- The server MUST send `stream_end` after every `user_message` it receives, including streams where output was suppressed (see [tool-responses.md](../hal-engine-tool-responses/spec.md)), streams that failed after an `error` frame, and a `user_message` that failed validation ([single-terminal-frame](../hal-engine-single-terminal-frame/spec.md)). The one exception is a turn whose socket closes first: it is abandoned, and nothing more is sent ([abandon-on-close](../hal-engine-abandon-on-close/spec.md)).
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:403:- `stream_end` follows every stream even after an error: a failed run sends its `error` frame first and `stream_end` last.
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md-404-- An `error` frame does not by itself end a stream. It is advisory: it may concern the run in flight or an unrelated frame, such as a malformed `ping`.
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md-405-- The client SHOULD use this message to clear any "processing" or "loading" indicators.
specs/hal-engine-websocket-protocol/spec.md:406:- The client MUST treat a run as complete only at `stream_end` or when the socket closes. Unparseable JSON is the one frame answered with `INVALID_FORMAT` and no `stream_end`, because the server cannot tell what it was meant to be; for it the client falls back on the socket closing.
